      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey quick question i'm doing my end of year bookkeeping for my freelance design work and i'm stuck on something. i just bought a really expensive ergonomic office chair for my studio setup. does that count as a business expense/deduction or is it considered a personal draw? trying to figure out how to categorize it in quickbooks. thanks",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The ergonomic office chair, a wise investment for your studio setup, indeed. You're right to wonder if it's a business expense or a personal draw. It's a bit like a tricky trap set by the IRS, but don't worry, I'm here to help you navigate it. Think of it like a trade-off between a comfortable workspace and a potentially taxable expense. \n\nIn QuickBooks, you can categorize it as a Business Expense, but you'll need to ensure it's not a Personal Use Expense. To do this, you'll need to keep a record of how you use the chair, and make sure it's primarily for business purposes. For example, you could document how many hours you spend working in the chair, or how often you use it for client meetings. \n\nIt's also worth noting that the IRS considers a chair to be a \"business use percentage\" item, which means you'll need to allocate a percentage of its cost to your business use. This is similar to how a business might allocate the cost of a car between personal and business use. \n\nTo make it easier, you can use the \"Business Use Percentage\" method, which involves calculating the percentage of time you use the chair for business purposes. For",
